1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"start-with-two","number":"08","title":"Let two people teach the general case.","mode":"Generality first","scale":"slide","dek":"Work through two players’ incentives before moving to the general network.","before":[{"kind":"image","title":"General game · PDF p. 2","image":"/originals/targeting-p02.png","alt":"A simultaneous investment game with n agents, standalone returns, strategic interaction, and a network matrix."},{"kind":"image","title":"Equilibrium condition · PDF p. 10","image":"/originals/targeting-p10.png","alt":"The planner’s general targeting problem and spectral-radius condition for unique equilibrium."}],"after":[{"title":"Each player responds to the other.","kind":"network","stage":1,"lines":["Each player chooses an investment.","An extra unit from the other player raises the best response by \\(\\tfrac14\\)."],"note":"\\(b_A=b_B=1\\) · \\(\\beta=\\tfrac14\\) · One reciprocal link","label":"A TWO-PLAYER NETWORK GAME","alt":"Two reciprocal links between A and B, each with coefficient one quarter. Each direct incentive equals one."},{"title":"Repeated responses amplify the initial incentive.","kind":"feedback","formula":"1+\\frac14+\\frac1{16}+\\frac1{64}+\\cdots=\\frac43","note":"\\(a_A=1+\\tfrac14a_B\\), \\(a_B=1+\\tfrac14a_A\\); hence \\(a_A=a_B=\\tfrac43\\).","label":"A TWO-PLAYER NETWORK GAME","alt":"Successive contributions: direct 1, first round 0.25, second 0.0625, third 0.015625. Cumulative responses approach 4/3."},{"kind":"equation","title":"The general equilibrium adds the same responses.","label":"INVESTMENT WITH SPILLOVERS","formula":"a^*=(I-\\beta g)^{-1}b=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(\\beta g)^k b","lines":["\\(b\\): direct incentives · \\(g\\): network · \\(\\beta\\): interaction strength","\\(k=0\\): direct incentives; \\(k=1,2,\\ldots\\): successive responses"],"note":"The expansion converges when \\(\\rho(\\beta g)<1\\)."}],"diagnosis":"The original presents an n-player payoff and then a general targeting problem. A solved two-player example gives the audience a concrete mechanism to recognize in the matrix notation.","changes":["Solve a small case with the mechanism intact.","Show the contribution of each round.","Carry the interpretation into the general notation."],"preserved":"The source’s payoff and equilibrium condition. The worked example sets \\(b=(1,1)\\), \\(\\beta=1/4\\), and \\(g_{AB}=g_{BA}=1\\), with no self-links.","deferred":"The general targeting problem and eigenvector characterization follow. The expansion uses the source’s spectral-radius condition.","practice":"Find the smallest example in which your main force still operates. Teach it, then say exactly what generalizes.","principle":"Choose detail that carries insight","sourceType":"public","context":"PDF p.2 introduces the general investment game; p.10 gives the targeting problem and spectral-radius condition. The two-player numbers and geometric-series display are derived specializations; the general equilibrium equation appears on p.36.","beforeCaption":"The general game and its equilibrium condition.","afterCaption":"A two-player calculation, followed by the general formula.","values":["Exact specialization: \\(b=(1,1)\\), \\(\\beta=1/4\\), and \\(g=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\).","First-order conditions give \\(a=b+\\beta ga\\); both actions equal \\(4/3\\).","The bars show terms in an equilibrium expansion, not a time series.","The two-player values are chosen for this reconstruction; the model and convergence condition come from the research talk."],"source":{"authors":"Andrea Galeotti, Benjamin Golub, Sanjeev Goyal","title":"Targeting interventions in networks","date":"2019","url":"https://bengolub.net/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/TIN_slides.pdf","pages":"2, 10; equilibrium equation 36"},"topic":"Network games","point":"Two reciprocal links make the amplification visible before the audience meets the matrix expression."}}]
